From 4c32fa177e0f94e7192595dd70378b753530ca02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: build <build@localhost>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 16:21:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Read a cue-size second voice on the main voice's stem as cue
 notes of its chord

A vocal line printed with a second singer's part in cue-size heads on the
tune's own stems (a harmony a third or a fourth above, a duet's lower voice a
third to a sixth below) came out as one wrong head per chord. With the small
heads switch off, small heads are never candidates; the void template
matched across the two heads (its ring's top on one, its bottom on the other,
the ledger between blanked) and that head between was the only one at the
stem's end, so the real head, its stem seed going on past it, was not an end
and went: [G5 + cue Bb5] read as an A5 half, quarters as halves, bars
overfull, the cue notes lost. Turning the switch on is worse: small heads are
then looked for everywhere (the curl of every quarter rest is one, a small
template fits inside a normal black head), a stem may not hold small and
standard heads together, a stem whose first head is small refuses a standard
flag, and a small head is exported as a grace note.

With the switch off, small heads are now cue partners only:
- HEADS: on each stem seed the small black and void templates are tried with
  the standard ones. A small head is kept only beside a standard head of the
  same kind on the same seed and side, 2 to 5 steps away, with a grade of
  0.45 or more, and no standard head at its pitch or a step away overlapping
  it with 0.75 of its grade or more (a small template inside or beside a real
  head). A kept one removes the weak standard heads on it or matched across
  the pair. Small seed heads are not competitors for the range scan.
- STEMS / REDUCTION: small heads are linked to stems and standard beams, and
  are not excluded by standard heads or standard beams on their stem.
- CHORDS: a stem with a standard head makes a standard chord, the cue head
  its member; a standard chord is told by its class, a grace stem by having
  no standard head (the flag is found again), a slur from a cue head names
  no grace chord.
- Export: the cue head is a <note> with <cue/> and <type size="cue">, after
  the chord's standard notes, with no <tie> element (<tied> stays).

On the owner's nine songs: Too Sweet 24 bars and Stumblin' In 9 bars of the
vocal line as printed (the merged heads, durations and cue notes of 49 of the
52 merged-head findings and 4 of the 14 missing-cue ones); the other seven
songs' MusicXML byte-identical; the HEADS step's time unchanged.

